            <ArticleTitle>Occipital neuralgia secondary to exuberant callus formation. Case report.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>1170-1</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>The authors report the case of a 78-year-old woman suffering from right occipital neuralgia in whom computerized tomography and magnetic resonance images demonstrated an irregular bone mass in the C-2 vertebral body. This &quot;bone tumor&quot; happened to be an exuberant callus formation that arose as a result of a previous axis body fracture. The patient's occipital pain was immediately relieved after she underwent C2-3 root release.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
